---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Notion API in Practice: Building a Task Management MCP Tool — Using AI Agents for Real Tasks"
  description="After organizing data, the next step is often to put it into Notion. This page walks through building a small Notion MCP server with three tools — read, create, update — so an AI agent can act on your classroom tasks within a clear, safe boundary."
  lang="en"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container ai-agent-notion">
      <a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; Back to Home</a>

      <header class="detail-header">
        <h1 class="detail-title">Build Your Own Small Notion MCP — Let AI Touch Notion Inside a Boundary</h1>
        <p class="detail-subtitle">
          We've already practiced having AI organize CSV and tabular data. But <strong>after the cleanup, there's almost always a next step</strong>: drop the to-do into Notion, flip a task to "In progress," mark a finished item as done. This page turns Notion into a tool an AI agent can use.
        </p>
        <p class="version-note">The steps and screens on this page were verified against Gemini CLI v0.54.4.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        We're <strong>not going to use an off-the-shelf Notion MCP</strong> here. Instead, we'll build a small classroom-style Notion MCP server ourselves. The benefits:
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>We get to <strong>decide what the AI can do</strong></li>
        <li>And <strong>what the AI can't do</strong></li>
        <li>The smaller the tool, the <strong>clearer its boundary</strong></li>
        <li>The clearer the boundary, the <strong>safer it is to use</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The Notion API lets external tools read, create, and update Notion data. But <strong>before any call you must create a connection</strong> and share the relevant page or database with it — otherwise the API has no permission. Notion also reminds developers that <strong>the token is a secret</strong>: never hardcode it or commit it to version control.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="detail-heading">Three things, no more</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This Notion tool is <strong>not an all-purpose Notion assistant</strong>. It only does three things:
      </p>

      <ul class="role-list">
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">list_classroom_tasks</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">read classroom tasks</span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">create_classroom_task</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">add a new classroom task</span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">update_task_status</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">update a task's status</span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Three actions are enough to practice the basics of the Notion API while <strong>keeping the tool small from day one</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- ============ PART 1 ============ -->
      <div class="part-divider">
        <span class="part-label">PART 1</span>
        <span class="part-text">Build a practice database in Notion</span>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Before writing any Python or MCP code, we need to set up a database in Notion. A Notion database is <strong>a slightly smarter table</strong> — each row holds field data and can also open into its own page. We recommend a <strong>Table database</strong> here: it's the closest thing to an ordinary table and the most natural fit for read / create / update practice.
      </p>

      <!-- STEP 1 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 1</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Create the database page</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Open Notion and do four things in order:
      </p>

      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">1</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Hover over the <strong>"Private"</strong> section in the sidebar and click <code>+</code> — or use the new-page button in the top-left — to create a new page</span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">2</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Title it <code>課堂任務管理</code> (Classroom Tasks)</span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">3</span><span class="numbered-list__text">In the empty body below the title, type <code>/database</code>（<code>/資料庫</code> in the Chinese UI）</span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">4</span><span class="numbered-list__text">When the menu appears, choose <strong>"Database - Full page"</strong>（「資料庫 - 整頁」）</span></li>
      </ol>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Notion側邊欄無資料庫入口.png"
          alt="The current Notion sidebar, showing only Home, Chat, Inbox, Search and a list of pages — with no Database entry"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 1. The Notion sidebar as it stands today. The top holds only Home, Chat, Inbox and Search, followed by the page list — there is no longer a "Database" entry. The older instruction to "click Database in the left sidebar" no longer applies; you create one from a new page's body using a slash command instead. (Screenshots on this page come from the Chinese-language run of the same exercise.)</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Notion斜線資料庫選單.png"
          alt="The filtered slash-command menu inside a Notion page body, listing Database - Inline, Database - Full page, and the table, board, gallery, list, feed, dashboard and calendar view modes"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 2. The filtered results after typing /資料庫. Only the top two entries actually create a database — pick the second one, "Database - Full page".</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>The menu is full of near-identical entries; only "Database - Full page" is the one you want.</strong><br/>
          ・"Database - Inline"（「資料庫 - 內嵌」）works too, but it sits inline with the page content, which makes it harder to follow along.<br/>
          ・"Table view", "Board view", "Gallery view" and the rest of that long list <strong>are ways of viewing data, not ways of creating a new database</strong>.<br/>
          ・The "Linked view of data source" entry at the bottom points at an <strong>existing</strong> data source — definitely not that one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 2 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 2</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Confirm the database and title column names</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        There are <strong>two names</strong> to check here, and both need changing:
      </p>

      <h4 class="example-heading">1. The database name</h4>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        A newly created database is called <strong>"New database"</strong>（「新資料庫」）by default. Rename it to <strong>「課堂任務管理」</strong>.
      </p>

      <h4 class="example-heading">2. The title column name</h4>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        A new database starts with <strong>exactly one column</strong> — the title column, called <strong>"Name"</strong>（「名稱」）and marked <code>Aa</code>. Click it and <strong>rename it to 「任務名稱」</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The title column's <strong>type cannot be changed</strong>, but its name can — and must be. The program reads <code>get_title(properties, "任務名稱")</code>; if the name doesn't match, it reads nothing, and all three tools come back with a blank task name.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The name is mostly <strong>for human readers</strong>. What the program actually uses is Notion's <code>data_source_id</code>, not the database name.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 3 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 3</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Create the columns</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Add the columns the database needs, exactly as listed below:
      </p>

      <div class="csv-table-wrapper">
        <table class="csv-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Column name</th>
              <th>Property type</th>
              <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>任務名稱</td><td>Title (already renamed in STEP 2 — <strong>don't create it again</strong>)</td><td>The task name</td></tr>
            <tr><td>類型</td><td>Select</td><td>Which category the task belongs to</td></tr>
            <tr><td>狀態</td><td>Select</td><td>Current processing status</td></tr>
            <tr><td>優先順序</td><td>Select</td><td>High / Medium / Low</td></tr>
            <tr><td>截止日</td><td>Date</td><td>When the task is due</td></tr>
            <tr><td>來源</td><td>Text</td><td>Where the task came from</td></tr>
            <tr><td>備註</td><td>Text</td><td>Additional notes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Skip the first row of that table.</strong>「任務名稱」 is the title column you already renamed back in STEP 2 — it was there all along, it just has a different name now. It's listed here so you can see the complete set of columns.<br/><br/>
          Create it a second time and the database ends up with two columns called 「任務名稱」 — one the title column, one a new text column. The program reads the title column, so all three tools come back with a blank task name, and nothing in the error message points at this. <strong>The columns you actually need to create are the six from the second row down.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Column names must match exactly.</strong> If the program uses「任務名稱」, don't shorten it to「任務」or「名稱」in Notion. The Notion API looks columns up <strong>by name</strong>; a mismatch means the program won't find the right field.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Take special care when adding the 「狀態」 column: the column is named 「狀態」, but its property type must be 「選取 / Select」.</strong> Not the property in that menu which is also called 「狀態 / Status」. The two sit right next to each other in the list and their icons look alike, but their API shapes are completely different — Status uses <code>"status"</code>, Select uses <code>"select"</code>. Pick the wrong one and the program reports an error, with no visible difference in the UI. This is the single most common place people get stuck on this page.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Notion屬性類型選取與狀態.png"
          alt="Notion's property-type menu when adding a column: the left column reads Text, Select, Status, Person, Checkbox, Phone; the right reads Number, Multi-select, Date, Files and media, URL, Email"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 3. The property-type menu when adding a column. 「選取 / Select」 and 「狀態 / Status」 sit one above the other in the left column and their icons look alike — this page needs 「選取 / Select」.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <!-- STEP 4 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 4</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Configure the Select options</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        A few columns aren't free-text — they use <strong>fixed options</strong>. This reduces the inconsistency problem (one person writes「處理中」, another「進行中」, another「正在處理」 — and the program can't tell). So we lock the options down up front. For each column below, use <strong>"Edit property"</strong> and add the options:
      </p>

      <h4 class="example-heading">「狀態」 column (3 options)</h4>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>待確認 (Pending)</li>
        <li>處理中 (In progress)</li>
        <li>已完成 (Done)</li>
      </ul>

      <h4 class="example-heading">「類型」 column (4 options)</h4>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>教學準備 (Teaching prep)</li>
        <li>學生回覆 (Student response)</li>
        <li>行政事項 (Admin)</li>
        <li>閱讀材料 (Reading material)</li>
      </ul>

      <h4 class="example-heading">「優先順序」 column (3 options)</h4>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>高 (High)</li>
        <li>中 (Medium)</li>
        <li>低 (Low)</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          These fixed options will line up with the Python program later —<br/>
          the tool <strong>only accepts these values</strong>.<br/>
          This isn't restricting the AI; it's <strong>making the data more stable</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 5 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 5</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Add some sample rows</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Once the columns are in place, add a few rows manually:
      </p>

      <div class="csv-table-wrapper">
        <table class="csv-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>任務名稱</th>
              <th>類型</th>
              <th>狀態</th>
              <th>優先順序</th>
              <th>截止日</th>
              <th>來源</th>
              <th>備註</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>確認下週課堂公告</td><td>教學準備</td><td>待確認</td><td>高</td><td>2026-09-20</td><td>課程安排</td><td>需要確認教室、時間與提醒內容</td></tr>
            <tr><td>整理學生提問</td><td>學生回覆</td><td>處理中</td><td>中</td><td>2026-09-22</td><td>課堂回饋</td><td>先整理重複問題</td></tr>
            <tr><td>準備閱讀材料摘要</td><td>閱讀材料</td><td>待確認</td><td>中</td><td>2026-09-25</td><td>閱讀清單</td><td>先產生摘要草稿，不直接發給學生</td></tr>
            <tr><td>回覆活動證明申請</td><td>行政事項</td><td>已完成</td><td>低</td><td>2026-09-18</td><td>活動報名表</td><td>已確認名單</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Notion課堂任務管理範例資料.png"
          alt="The 課堂任務管理 database in table view, with columns for task name, type, status, priority, due date, source and notes, holding four sample rows"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 4. What PART 1 looks like when it's done. All seven columns are in place, the four sample rows are filled in, and 「類型」「狀態」「優先順序」 all render as coloured select tags. The Notion side is ready.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        These rows are what later exercises will use. It's a small dataset, but a useful one — it covers <strong>different statuses, types, and priorities</strong>, plus tasks that can be updated.
      </p>

      <!-- ============ PART 2 ============ -->
      <div class="part-divider">
        <span class="part-label">PART 2</span>
        <span class="part-text">Set up a connection to the Notion API</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="detail-heading">First: database vs. data source</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        In the newer Notion API design, <strong>database and data source are separate concepts</strong>. A simple way to think about it:
      </p>

      <ul class="role-list">
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">Database</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">Behaves more like a <strong>shell or container</strong></span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">Data source</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">The actual <strong>table that holds each row</strong></span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">Page</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">Each row in the API is treated as a "page"</span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Notion's docs note that starting from <strong>API version 2025-09-03</strong>, most operations that used to take <code>database_id</code> now take <code>data_source_id</code>; <code>2026-03-11</code> is also available as an API header. The examples on this page use the <strong>new data_source_id form</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So we need two key pieces of information:
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><code>NOTION_API_KEY</code></li>
        <li><code>NOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID</code></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- STEP A -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP A</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Create a Notion connection</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        For Python to reach Notion, we first create a connection. From the workspace switcher in the top-left of Notion, go to <strong>Settings → Connections</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That page has three tabs across the top — Explore, Installed, Manage — but <strong>none of those is what you want</strong>. Click <strong>"Go to developer portal ↗" on the right</strong>.
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Notion設定連接頁面.png"
          alt="The Connections page in Notion settings, with Explore, Installed and Manage tabs, a grid of connectors such as Google Calendar and Slack, and a link to the developer portal on the right"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 5. The Settings → Connections screen. The large grid in the middle is the ready-made connectors, none of which we need here — the link to click is "Go to developer portal ↗" on the right.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        In the developer portal, create a new integration. The <strong>"New connection"</strong> dialog has three fields to fill in:
      </p>

      <ul class="role-list">
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">Name</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">Enter <code>Classroom Tool</code></span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">Auth method</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">Choose <strong>"Access token"</strong> — a static, workspace-scoped API token shared by collaborators, limited to one workspace</span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">Can be installed in</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">Pick your own workspace</span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Notion新連接對話框.png"
          alt="Notion's New connection dialog with Classroom Tool entered as the name and two auth options, Access token and OAuth, with Access token selected"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 6. The New connection dialog. For the auth method, pick the upper option, "Access token".</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The other option, <strong>OAuth</strong>, is for apps that will be listed on the Marketplace and support multiple workspaces. We don't need it here.<br/><br/>
          The difference between the two echoes something from the fundamentals: <strong>granting an account is not the same as granting a token</strong>. With OAuth, each signed-in user authorizes separately and permissions follow the person; an access token is a fixed key — whoever holds it can use it. That's exactly why it belongs in <code>.env</code> and never in the source code.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP B -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP B</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Set the connection's permissions</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Creating it <strong>does not drop you into its settings page</strong>. Go to <strong>"Connections" in the left sidebar</strong> and click into the <code>Classroom Tool</code> you just made — that's where the token and the capability settings live.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The <strong>"Capabilities"</strong> block on that page has three groups, and all three need a look:
      </p>

      <ul class="role-list">
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">Content</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">Read content, update content, insert content — <strong>already checked by default; leave them on</strong></span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">Comment</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">Read comments, insert comments — <strong>leave them off</strong></span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">User</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">One of three, defaulting to <strong>"Read user information including email addresses"</strong> — <strong>you must change it to the topmost option, "No user information"</strong></span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Notion連接功能權限設定.png"
          alt="The Classroom Tool settings page, showing the Capabilities block split into content, comment and user capabilities, with the user group defaulting to reading user information including email addresses"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 7. The "Capabilities" block on the connection settings page. The three content capabilities come pre-checked and the comment ones stay off; below them, the three user-capability options run from narrowest to widest — and Notion pre-selects the widest.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>The user capability defaults to the widest setting, and you have to narrow it yourself.</strong> This tool has no use for user data — it only reads and writes columns in the 「課堂任務管理」 database. It doesn't need to know who anyone is, and it certainly doesn't need email addresses. Change it to <strong>"No user information"</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          Three options, narrowest to widest, side by side on one screen —<br/>
          and the default isn't the narrowest.<br/>
          <strong>Which one should you pick, and why isn't the narrowest the default?</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>Don't enable more than you need.</strong> This tool only reads, creates, and updates tasks — there's no need for delete, and no need to manage the entire workspace.
      </p>

      <!-- STEP C -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP C</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Share the database with the connection</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        After creating the connection, <strong>it isn't usable yet</strong>. You need to go back to the「課堂任務管理」database and share it with the connection — otherwise API requests will fail.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Open「課堂任務管理」 in Notion, then:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>Top-right ⋯ → Connections → Select Classroom Tool → Add to page</code></pre>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That last <strong>"Add to page"</strong> has to be clicked for it to take effect — selecting <code>Classroom Tool</code> on its own isn't enough.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>This step is critical.</strong> A lot of Notion API errors aren't bugs in the code — they're <strong>databases that haven't been shared with the connection</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP D -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP D</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Find the data source ID</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The simplest path: in the database's top-right area, click the slider icon labelled <strong>"Settings"</strong> (left of "New"), then:
      </p>
      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">1</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Click <strong>"Manage data sources"</strong></span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">2</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Click the <strong>⋯</strong> next to「課堂任務管理」</span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">3</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Copy the <strong>data source ID</strong></span></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Set this ID aside — we'll need it shortly.
      </p>

      <!-- ============ PART 3 ============ -->
      <div class="part-divider">
        <span class="part-label">PART 3</span>
        <span class="part-text">Write a small MCP server</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="detail-heading">Before you start: check that uv works</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Everything from here runs in PowerShell, and it needs <code>uv</code>. Confirm it's installed:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>uv --version</code></pre>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If the command isn't found, install it first:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>powershell -ExecutionPolicy ByPass -c "irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex"</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Installing takes about <strong>30 seconds</strong> in practice, needs <strong>no administrator rights</strong>, and lands in <code>%USERPROFILE%\.local\bin</code>. You may need to <strong>open a fresh PowerShell window</strong> afterwards before the command is found.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP I -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP I</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Create the project folder</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        In PowerShell, run these in order:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>cd $HOME
mkdir notion-classroom-tool
cd notion-classroom-tool
uv init</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Once <code>uv init</code> finishes, the folder looks like this:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>notion-classroom-tool
├─ .git/            ← uv init also initializes a git repository
├─ .gitignore       ← written by uv itself
├─ .python-version
├─ README.md
├─ main.py          ← not used on this page; just leave it
└─ pyproject.toml</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Running <code>uv add</code> later adds <code>.venv/</code> and <code>uv.lock</code> on top of these. <code>main.py</code> is the sample file <code>uv init</code> ships with — this page never touches it, so <strong>there's no need to delete or edit it</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Open <code>pyproject.toml</code> and look at the <code>requires-python</code> line. <strong>Its value follows whichever Python is on your machine</strong> — we've seen both <code>&gt;=3.14</code> and <code>&gt;=3.10</code> in testing. <strong>If it isn't <code>&gt;=3.10</code>, change it to <code>&gt;=3.10</code></strong> to avoid a version conflict in the next step.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP II -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP II</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Create the three config files</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This step creates three files: <code>.env</code>, <code>.gitignore</code> and <code>.gemini\.env</code>. <strong>Do all three before moving on</strong> — that way there's nothing to come back for.
      </p>

      <h4 class="example-heading">1. Set up .env</h4>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Get two values ready:
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>The <code>notion_connection_token</code> appears as <strong>"Internal Integration Secret"</strong> on the Classroom Tool page after creation — click "Show" to reveal it before copying</li>
        <li>The <code>data_source_id</code> is the one you just copied in STEP D</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Then create the file from PowerShell:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>Set-Content -Path ".env" -Value "NOTION_API_KEY=your_token`nNOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID=your_id`nNOTION_VERSION=2026-03-11"</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Don't create <code>.env</code> in Notepad.</strong> Notepad quietly appends <code>.txt</code> on save, turning it into <code>.env.txt</code>, and the program won't find it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Important:<br/>
          ✗ Don't <strong>hardcode the API key</strong> in Python.<br/>
          ✗ Don't <strong>push .env to GitHub</strong>.<br/>
          ✗ Don't <strong>paste the token into public docs</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h4 class="example-heading">NOTION_VERSION is a fixed identifier, not today's date</h4>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <code>NOTION_VERSION</code> looks like a date, but it <strong>isn't "today"</strong> — it's an <strong>API version identifier</strong> that Notion has published. Only a handful of values are legal; any other date is rejected, <strong>including future ones</strong>. Copy <code>2026-03-11</code> exactly (it's the newest, and the one that supports the <code>data_source_id</code> form).
        </p>
      </div>

      <h4 class="example-heading">2. .gitignore</h4>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>uv init</code> already wrote a <code>.gitignore</code> for you, but <strong>it doesn't list <code>.env</code></strong>. Overwrite it with the three entries that matter here:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>Set-Content -Path ".gitignore" -Value ".env`n.venv/`n__pycache__/"</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          This isn't optional — <code>uv init</code> also ran <code>git init</code>, so <strong>this folder is already a git repository</strong>. Commit without <code>.env</code> in <code>.gitignore</code> and the token goes straight into version history.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h4 class="example-heading">3. .gemini\.env</h4>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This project folder <strong>needs its own Gemini key and model setting</strong> too, otherwise the CLI will show an auth menu when you launch it later:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>mkdir .gemini
Set-Content -Path ".gemini\.env" -Value "GEMINI_API_KEY=your_key`nGEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite"</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Verified in testing: with this file present, the CLI's start-up screen shows <code>Authenticated with gemini-api-key</code> directly and <strong>no auth menu appears</strong>. If one does appear, the file wasn't created properly or is in the wrong place.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP III -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP III</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Install dependencies</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The project was already initialized with <code>uv init</code> in STEP I, so go straight to installing. <strong>Copy this exactly, version range included</strong>:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>uv add "mcp[cli]&gt;=1.0,&lt;2.0" python-dotenv requests</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Why pin the version?</strong><br/><br/>
          Without a version range, <code>uv add</code> pulls the newest release every time. The mcp package removed <code>mcp.server.fastmcp</code> entirely in 2.0, so the import on line 5 of the program fails outright with <code>ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'mcp.server.fastmcp'</code>.<br/><br/>
          This is the hardest kind of error to diagnose yourself, because it looks like a typo. In fact not a character of the program is wrong — <strong>the package changed under it</strong>.<br/><br/>
          Pinning to the lower range tells the machine: this material was written against 1.x, please install 1.x.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Once it's installed, <strong>confirm the version immediately</strong> rather than waiting until you run the program:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>uv pip show mcp
uv run python -c "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP; print('ok')"</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The first line should report a version starting with 1; the second should print <code>ok</code>, meaning the import resolves. <strong>Both must pass before you continue.</strong>
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>If you hit a <code>requires-python</code> conflict here</strong> (the message will say it can't find a combination satisfying both <code>mcp&lt;2.0</code> and your Python version), the <code>pyproject.toml</code> from STEP I hasn't been edited yet. Go back, set <code>requires-python</code> to <code>&gt;=3.10</code>, and run <code>uv add</code> again.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP IV -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP IV</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Which tools are we building?</h2>
      </div>

      <ul class="role-list">
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">list_classroom_tasks</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc"><strong>Reads Notion tasks</strong></span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">create_classroom_task</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc"><strong>Creates a new Notion task</strong></span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">update_task_status</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc"><strong>Updates a task's status</strong></span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          We're <strong>deliberately not building a delete tool</strong>. Delete is a high-risk action and not appropriate for this practice.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP V -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP V</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Write the MCP server</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The program runs to about 220 lines, which is painful to paste line by line in PowerShell. Open Notepad instead:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>notepad notion_classroom_server.py</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Choose <strong>Yes</strong> when it asks whether to create a new file, paste in the full program below, then save and close.
      </p>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">notion_classroom_server.py — full source</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click to expand (~220 lines)</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-python">from typing import Optional
import os
import requests
from dotenv import load_dotenv
from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP

load_dotenv()

mcp = FastMCP("notion-classroom")

NOTION_API_KEY = os.getenv("NOTION_API_KEY")
NOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID = os.getenv("NOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID")
NOTION_VERSION = os.getenv("NOTION_VERSION", "2026-03-11")

NOTION_BASE_URL = "https://api.notion.com/v1"

ALLOWED_STATUS = ["待確認", "處理中", "已完成"]
ALLOWED_CATEGORY = ["教學準備", "學生回覆", "行政事項", "閱讀材料"]
ALLOWED_PRIORITY = ["高", "中", "低"]


def notion_headers() -> dict:
    if not NOTION_API_KEY:
        raise ValueError("Missing NOTION_API_KEY. Check your .env file.")

    return {
        "Authorization": f"Bearer {NOTION_API_KEY}",
        "Notion-Version": NOTION_VERSION,
        "Content-Type": "application/json",
    }


def notion_request(method: str, path: str, payload: Optional[dict] = None) -> dict:
    try:
        response = requests.request(
            method=method,
            url=f"{NOTION_BASE_URL}{path}",
            headers=notion_headers(),
            json=payload,
            timeout=20,
        )

        if response.status_code >= 400:
            try:
                detail = response.json()
            except Exception:
                detail = response.text

            return {
                "ok": False,
                "status_code": response.status_code,
                "message": "Notion API returned an error.",
                "detail": detail,
            }

        return {
            "ok": True,
            "data": response.json(),
        }

    except requests.RequestException as error:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "Failed to reach the Notion API.",
            "detail": str(error),
        }


def get_title(properties: dict, name: str) -> str:
    title_items = properties.get(name, {}).get("title", [])
    return "".join(item.get("plain_text", "") for item in title_items)


def get_select(properties: dict, name: str) -> str:
    value = properties.get(name, {}).get("select")
    if not value:
        return ""
    return value.get("name", "")


def get_date(properties: dict, name: str) -> str:
    value = properties.get(name, {}).get("date")
    if not value:
        return ""
    return value.get("start", "")


def get_rich_text(properties: dict, name: str) -> str:
    text_items = properties.get(name, {}).get("rich_text", [])
    return "".join(item.get("plain_text", "") for item in text_items)


def page_to_task(page: dict) -> dict:
    properties = page.get("properties", {})

    return {
        "page_id": page.get("id", ""),
        "url": page.get("url", ""),
        "任務名稱": get_title(properties, "任務名稱"),
        "類型": get_select(properties, "類型"),
        "狀態": get_select(properties, "狀態"),
        "優先順序": get_select(properties, "優先順序"),
        "截止日": get_date(properties, "截止日"),
        "來源": get_rich_text(properties, "來源"),
        "備註": get_rich_text(properties, "備註"),
    }


@mcp.tool()
def list_classroom_tasks(status: str = "待確認", limit: int = 10) -> dict:
    """
    Read classroom tasks from Notion.

    status can be: 全部, 待確認, 處理中, 已完成.
    limit is the max number of rows returned, between 1 and 20.
    """
    if not NOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "Missing NOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID. Check your .env file.",
        }

    if status not in ["全部"] + ALLOWED_STATUS:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "Status not allowed.",
            "allowed_status": ["全部"] + ALLOWED_STATUS,
        }

    if limit &lt; 1 or limit &gt; 20:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "limit must be between 1 and 20.",
        }

    payload = {
        "page_size": limit,
    }

    if status != "全部":
        payload["filter"] = {
            "property": "狀態",
            "select": {
                "equals": status,
            },
        }

    result = notion_request(
        "POST",
        f"/data_sources/{NOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID}/query",
        payload,
    )

    if not result["ok"]:
        return result

    pages = result["data"].get("results", [])
    tasks = [page_to_task(page) for page in pages]

    return {
        "ok": True,
        "count": len(tasks),
        "tasks": tasks,
    }


@mcp.tool()
def create_classroom_task(
    title: str,
    category: str = "教學準備",
    priority: str = "中",
    due_date: str = "",
    source: str = "",
    note: str = "",
) -> dict:
    """
    Create a classroom task in Notion.

    title is the task name.
    category can be: 教學準備, 學生回覆, 行政事項, 閱讀材料.
    priority can be: 高, 中, 低.
    due_date should use YYYY-MM-DD format, e.g. 2026-09-20.
    """
    if not NOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "Missing NOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID. Check your .env file.",
        }

    title = title.strip()

    if not title:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "Task name cannot be empty.",
        }

    if len(title) &gt; 80:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "Task name is too long. Keep it under 80 characters.",
        }

    if category not in ALLOWED_CATEGORY:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "Category not allowed.",
            "allowed_category": ALLOWED_CATEGORY,
        }

    if priority not in ALLOWED_PRIORITY:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "Priority not allowed.",
            "allowed_priority": ALLOWED_PRIORITY,
        }

    properties = {
        "任務名稱": {
            "title": [{"text": {"content": title}}],
        },
        "類型": {
            "select": {"name": category},
        },
        "狀態": {
            "select": {"name": "待確認"},
        },
        "優先順序": {
            "select": {"name": priority},
        },
        "來源": {
            "rich_text": [{"text": {"content": source}}],
        },
        "備註": {
            "rich_text": [{"text": {"content": note}}],
        },
    }

    if due_date:
        properties["截止日"] = {
            "date": {"start": due_date},
        }

    payload = {
        "parent": {
            "type": "data_source_id",
            "data_source_id": NOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID,
        },
        "properties": properties,
    }

    result = notion_request("POST", "/pages", payload)

    if not result["ok"]:
        return result

    page = result["data"]

    return {
        "ok": True,
        "message": "Notion task created.",
        "page_id": page.get("id", ""),
        "url": page.get("url", ""),
        "任務名稱": title,
        "狀態": "待確認",
    }


@mcp.tool()
def update_task_status(page_id: str, new_status: str) -> dict:
    """
    Update a Notion task's status.

    new_status can only be: 待確認, 處理中, 已完成.
    This tool only updates status — it never modifies the title, date, or notes.
    """
    page_id = page_id.strip()

    if not page_id:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "Missing page_id. Cannot update task.",
        }

    if new_status not in ALLOWED_STATUS:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "message": "Status not allowed.",
            "allowed_status": ALLOWED_STATUS,
        }

    payload = {
        "properties": {
            "狀態": {
                "select": {"name": new_status},
            },
        },
    }

    result = notion_request("PATCH", f"/pages/{page_id}", payload)

    if not result["ok"]:
        return result

    return {
        "ok": True,
        "message": "Task status updated.",
        "page_id": page_id,
        "new_status": new_status,
    }


if __name__ == "__main__":
    mcp.run()
</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

