      <!-- STEP VII -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP VII</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Start it on its own first</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Before wiring it into Gemini CLI, <strong>run the program by itself</strong> to confirm it's sound:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>uv run python notion_classroom_server.py</code></pre>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>The correct result is: no output at all, cursor sitting there.</strong><br/>
          Press Ctrl+C to stop.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Nothing happening is the right outcome. Once an MCP server starts, it simply waits for Gemini CLI to connect — it was never going to print anything.<br/><br/>
          If you get <code>ModuleNotFoundError</code> here, <strong>the packages are the problem</strong>; if you get <code>ValueError: 缺少 NOTION_API_KEY</code>, <strong><code>.env</code> isn't being read</strong>. <strong>Solve it here before going further.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Don't skip this step.</strong> If you go straight to Gemini CLI, a failure shows up only as an empty <code>/mcp list</code>, with nothing to go on — packages? <code>.env</code>? a broken <code>settings.json</code>? Testing here rules out one possibility at a time.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP VIII -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP VIII</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Launch the CLI and verify</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Start it from inside the project folder:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>npx @google/gemini-cli</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          On start-up it asks whether you trust this folder — <strong>choose <code>1. Trust folder</code></strong>. This is a new folder, so it will ask again. Choose wrong and <code>settings.json</code> is never loaded, leaving <code>/mcp list</code> empty — and the screen won't tell you why.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Check that the <strong>status bar in the bottom-right</strong> reads <code>gemini-3.5-flash-lite</code>, then run:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>/mcp list</code></pre>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If everything's wired correctly, you should see <code>notion-classroom</code> as an MCP server, along with the <strong>three tools</strong> we just defined.
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Notion練習mcp清單.png"
          alt="The Gemini CLI mcp list screen showing two MCP servers, notion and notion-classroom, the latter marked Ready with three tools listed"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 8. The <code>/mcp list</code> screen. <code>notion-classroom</code> shows Ready (3 tools) with the full name of each tool below it. The <code>notion</code> entry above it is the official Notion MCP; whether it shows up depends on which layer it was installed at — seeing only <code>notion-classroom</code> is perfectly normal too.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="detail-heading">If you see two MCP servers</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Gemini CLI reads MCP configuration from <strong>two layers</strong>, and they stack rather than replace one another:
      </p>

      <div class="csv-table-wrapper">
        <table class="csv-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Layer</th>
              <th>Path</th>
              <th>When it has anything in it</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>User</td><td><code>%USERPROFILE%\.gemini\settings.json</code></td><td>Only if you once set an MCP server up at this level by hand</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Project</td><td><code>project-folder\.gemini\settings.json</code></td><td>Where <code>gemini mcp add</code> writes by default — and it only applies inside that folder</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So the <code>/mcp list</code> you just ran <strong>may not look the same for everyone</strong>:
      </p>

      <ul class="role-list">
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">Just one</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">Only <code>notion-classroom</code> — the simplest case, so <strong>go straight on to the exercises</strong>. If the official Notion MCP you connected earlier was written into a different project folder, it won't load in this one.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label">Both of them</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc">Both <code>notion</code> and <code>notion-classroom</code> — meaning the official one sits at the user layer, or you once installed it in this project too. <strong>Disable it temporarily</strong> before going on.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h4 class="example-heading">If both are present, why disable the official one first</h4>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Because with two sets of tools that can both act on Notion, the model may reach for the official one. The task does get done and the Notion data does look right — but <strong>your own tool was never actually wired up correctly, and you can't tell</strong>. The point of this page is to verify the thing you built, so set the other one aside first.
      </p>

      <h4 class="example-heading">How to disable it</h4>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>Fully close Gemini CLI first</strong>, or the file will be locked and the rename will fail. Then look at both paths to find where <code>notion</code> is actually configured:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>Get-Content "$HOME\.gemini\settings.json"
Get-Content ".gemini\settings.json"</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Whichever file contains <code>"notion"</code> is the one to change. <strong>Usually it's the first</strong>:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>Rename-Item -Path "$HOME\.gemini\settings.json" -NewName "settings.json.bak"</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          If it's at the <strong>project layer</strong> (the second file), <strong>don't rename the whole thing</strong> — that file also holds the <code>notion-classroom</code> you just wrote, and renaming takes both out. In that case open the file directly, delete the <code>notion</code> block, and leave <code>notion-classroom</code> in place.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Restart the CLI afterwards and run <code>/mcp list</code> to confirm only <code>notion-classroom</code> remains.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Rename it back when you're done with the exercises:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code>Rename-Item -Path "$HOME\.gemini\settings.json.bak" -NewName "settings.json"</code></pre>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          Worth noting as a comparison:<br/>
          the official Notion MCP exposes <strong>28 tools</strong> when unrestricted,<br/>
          while ours has <strong>3</strong>.<br/>
          Fewer tools means <strong>less description to read, and fewer tokens per call</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ PART 4 ============ -->
      <div class="part-divider">
        <span class="part-label">PART 4</span>
        <span class="part-text">Prompt Exercises</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Exercise 1 -->
